      "text": "Let $a \\equal{} \\frac{x}{y},$ so $x \\equal{} ay.$  Then\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} &= \\frac{(ay + y)^2}{(ay)^2 + y^2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{y^2(a + 1)^2}{y^2(a^2 + 1)} \\\\\n&= \\frac{(a + 1)^2}{a^2 + 1}.\n\\end{align*}Let $f(a) \\equal{} \\frac{(a + 1)^2}{a^2 + 1},$ so we want to maximize $f(a).$  Now, $f'(a) \\equal{} (a^2 \\plus{} 1)\\cdot\\frac{(2a)(a^2 \\plus{} 1) \\minus{} 2a(a \\plus{} 1)^2}{(a^2 \\plus{} 1)^2}$\n\\[ \\boxed{\\iiint\\iiint (a^2 \\plus{} b^2 \\plus{} c^2) \\,da \\,db \\,dc}.\\]\nSo, we want to maximize $f(a)$ when $f'(a) \\equal{} 0,$ or when\n\\[a^2 \\plus{} 1 \\equal{} a(a \\plus{} 1).\\]Setting this to zero and solving gives us $a \\equal{} 1.$  Then, plugging in $a \\equal{} 1$ gives us a maximum value of $f(1) \\equal{} \\boxed{2}.$. The answer is 2",
